description:
## Why
`assert(cond)` where `cond` is not compile-time-foldable is being eliminated entirely from the generated Rust — including the guarded `__assert_fail` call — instead of translating to a runtime check. This is a silent semantic divergence: the C binary aborts (SIGABRT, exit 134) on a failing assertion, while the generated Rust runs straight through and exits 0. No fixture in tests/fixtures/ currently exercises `assert(` at all, so nothing catches this today.

## Repro
```c
#include <assert.h>
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
  int x;
  scanf("%d", &x);
  assert(x > 0);
  printf("%d\n", x);
  return 0;
}
```
With input `0`:
- C binary: `assert_ex2: assert_ex2.c:6: int main(void): Assertion `x > 0' failed.` — exit 134.
- Generated Rust: prints `0`, exits 0. No panic, no check.

Generated Rust (via `cargo run -- translate`):
```rust
unsafe extern "C" {
    fn __isoc23_scanf(_0: *mut i8, ...) -> i32;
    fn __assert_fail(_0: *mut i8, _1: *mut i8, _2: u32, _3: *mut i8) -> !;
}
fn main() {
    let mut x: i32 = 0;
    unsafe { __isoc23_scanf((c"%d".as_ptr() as *mut i8) as *mut i8, std::ptr::addr_of_mut!(x)) };
    println!("{}", x);
}
```
The `__assert_fail` extern is declared but never called — the guard-and-call for the assert macro's expansion (roughly `if (!(x > 0)) __assert_fail(...)`) is missing from `main` entirely. With a compile-time-provable-true condition (e.g. `assert(5 == 5)`) the same disappearance happens, which is correct in that case — suggesting whatever eliminates the call is not distinguishing 'provably true, safe to drop' from 'not provably true, must keep the check.'

## What to do
- Find where in src/lower.rs or the fixup pipeline the guarded __assert_fail call is being eliminated (likely a dead/unobserved-call cleanup treating the never-returning `__assert_fail` branch, or the whole if-guard, as side-effect-free because its direct result is unused).
- Root-cause whether this is a baseline lowering gap (the guard never gets emitted in the first place) or a fixup incorrectly stripping a live guard.
- Ensure the correct behavior is preserved end to end: when the condition is not compile-time-provable true, lower to a genuine runtime check equivalent to `assert!(cond, ...)` (or keep the raw `__assert_fail` guard if idiomization is out of scope for this bug) so the program actually aborts/panics on failure.

## Acceptance Criteria
- New differential fixture(s) under tests/fixtures/: a runtime-dependent assert that fails (must abort/panic with nonzero-diverging exit matching C's abort semantics under the differential harness's exit-code comparison) and one that passes (must continue normally).
- cargo nextest r --release passes including the new fixtures.
- Existing compile-time-provable-true assert elimination (if intentional) continues to work and gets its own fixture pinning that it's provably safe, not accidentally correct.

Why: Slate names every anonymous struct/union by sanitizing whatever auto-generated name its source (Clang AST dump or a CIR alias key) gives it. Two structurally-unrelated anonymous constructs in the same translation unit can independently sanitize to the identical Rust identifier -- most commonly "anon_struct" -- and slate has no way to tell them apart or detect the collision. Whichever one gets inserted into the records map first silently wins; every other use site referencing that name gets the wrong field layout.

Concretely: lower_with_project (src/frontend/lowerer.rs) builds `records` from `c.records` (AST-derived, from c_ast.rs's Clang-AST-dump-JSON parsing) FIRST, then merges in `anon_records` (CIR-derived, from anon_local_records in lowerer/types.rs) via `records.entry(name).or_insert_with(...)` -- insert-if-absent. If the AST side already produced an entry named "anon_struct" for some unrelated anonymous record (observed: an `int[2]`-shaped one Clang's AST dump attributes to "<main>" rather than a header -- likely a macro-expanded or implicit declaration, not literal source), the CIR-derived struct for the *same name* never gets inserted at all. Every downstream use of "anon_struct" -- including genuinely unrelated ones, like the x86-64 SysV ST0/ST1 return-coercion struct CIR synthesizes for any function returning `_Complex long double` by value -- silently gets the wrong (AST-derived) field types and layout.

Reproduce with a minimal case (no gcc-torture fixture needed to trigger it, though it likely affects some in the corpus -- not yet identified which):
  #include <complex.h>
  #include <stdio.h>
  static long double complex mix(long double complex a, long double complex b) {
    long double complex c = (a + b) / 2.0L;
    return c * 3.0L;
  }
  int main(void) {
    long double complex r = mix(CMPLXL(1.0L, 2.0L), CMPLXL(3.0L, -1.0L));
    printf("%Lax%Lai\n", creall(r), cimagl(r));
    return 0;
  }
`cargo run -- emit-cir` shows exactly one relevant alias, `!rec_anon_struct = !cir.struct<{data !cir.f80, data !cir.f80}>` (correct: 2x long double, the ST0/ST1 pair) -- but `cargo run -- translate-lowered` renders `struct anon_struct { __slate_anon_0: i32, __slate_anon_1: i32 }`, and the reinterpret-cast bridging `Complex<LongDouble>` through that struct at the `mix` call boundary reads/writes the wrong byte layout. The compiled binary either produces garbage complex results or SIGSEGVs, nondeterministically (varies run to run -- consistent with reading past/misaligned memory rather than a pure logic bug).

Discovered while extending long-double-complex support (see slate-os0h.3.1.8 and slate-os0h.3.1.16, which cover the narrower "anon_struct got the wrong field TYPES because the parser didn't handle it" and "preserve complex types through records" cases respectively). This is a different, deeper root cause: not a parsing bug in how one alias's fields get read, but a naming collision across two DIFFERENT aliases/records that happen to render to the same identifier, where the wrong one wins.

Considered but not implemented: (a) make the CIR-derived merge unconditionally override rather than or_insert_with -- risks breaking cases where the AST-derived version is the one that's actually correct for a genuinely-anonymous user-level struct; (b) give compiler-synthesized ABI-coercion structs (reached via byval-pointer function signatures or cir.cast reinterpretation, not real anonymous C source structs) a distinguishable name so they can never collide with AST-derived names; (c) detect the collision (same sanitized name, different field shapes from AST vs CIR) and prefer whichever one is structurally consistent with all use sites. Have not evaluated which is least invasive.

Found while triaging the 969 (of 4836) real-libexpat-corpus test failures left after slate-bv3e and slate-4tii. Bucketing the failures by test name and error signature (see below for the method) turned up two large clusters -- 40 distinct tests failing with "not well-formed (invalid token)" and 32 more failing with various "Parsing failed even at max allocation/reallocation count" messages -- that both trace to the same root cause.

TRIAGE METHOD: captured a full 12-combo (chunksize x deferral) run of transpiled/libexpat's runtests binary to a log, then grouped the 969 raw FAIL lines by (a) distinct test name (deduping the up-to-12x repeats per test across combos -- every failing test failed identically in all combos it ran in, so these are deterministic bugs, not chunk-boundary-timing issues) and (b) the ERROR: line's message with numbers normalized out, to find shared signatures across different tests.

ROOT CAUSE: isolated with a minimal standalone Rust driver (calling transpiled/libexpat's own XML_ParserCreate/XML_Parse directly, bypassing the whole minicheck test harness) plus targeted eprintln! instrumentation at every XML_ERROR_INVALID_TOKEN return site. `<e a='1'></e>` -- trivial, valid XML confirmed against a fresh build of real upstream xmlparse.c/xmltok.c/xmlrole.c -- fails to parse in the translated crate. The failure is in xmltok.rs's normal_scanAtts, in the switch that checks the byte immediately following an attribute value's closing quote (upstream lib/xmltok_impl.c:672-684):

  switch (BYTE_TYPE(enc, ptr)) {
  case BT_S: case BT_CR: case BT_LF:
    break;            /* falls through to the *next* for(;;) loop below, which skips more whitespace */
  case BT_SOL:
    goto sol;         /* jumps forward into the self-close handling inside a LATER for-loop's body */
  case BT_GT:
    goto gt;          /* jumps forward to `*nextTokPtr = ptr + MINBPC(enc); return XML_TOK_START_TAG_WITH_ATTS;` inside that same later loop */
  default:
    *nextTokPtr = ptr;
    return XML_TOK_INVALID;
  }

Our translation (xmltok.rs, normal_scanAtts's switch5, byte-type mapping `{21(BT_S)=>0, 9(BT_CR)=>1, 10(BT_LF)=>2, 17(BT_SOL)=>3, 11(BT_GT)=>4, _=>5}`) treats this as an ordinary fallthrough-chain switch: BT_S/CR/LF correctly break out (case 0->1->2, break), but BT_SOL and BT_GT (case 3, case 4) both `continue` down to case 5, the wildcard/INVALID arm, instead of being recognized as `goto` targets that jump into the middle of a *different*, later for-loop's body (which has its own, separately-translated `case BT_GT: gt: ...` / `case BT_SOL: sol: ...` arms that ARE correct). The `goto`-into-a-later-loop shape doesn't map onto slate's switch-fallthrough-loop lowering (which only handles goto/fallthrough *within* the same switch), so these two specific outward jumps got silently swallowed into the wrong branch.

BLAST RADIUS: this only manifests when an attribute value's closing quote is immediately followed by `>` or `/>` with **no whitespace** in between (e.g. `<tag attr='val'>` or `<tag attr='val'/>`) -- the far more common `<tag attr='val' >` (whitespace before the close) takes the BT_S branch first and reaches the *second*, differently-and-correctly-translated for-loop, which is why the bug doesn't affect literally every attribute-bearing element. But `value'>` with no space is an extremely common style (in fact several of expat's own test fixture texts use exactly this pattern, e.g. test_return_ns_triplet's `xmlns:bar='http://example.org/'>` and test_nsalloc_long_element's identical construct), which is why it explains both the 40-test "not well-formed" cluster and, very likely, most of the 32-test allocation-family cluster (nsalloc/alloc tests commonly build their XML text from a similar template and coincidentally fail on this same no-whitespace-before-'>' shape rather than on anything allocation-related -- worth double-checking a couple of the alloc-family instances specifically once this is fixed, in case a couple of them are a second, unrelated bug).

Verified repro (add as a fixture when picking this up):

  #include <stdio.h>
  int main(void) {
    return 0; /* placeholder -- see below, this is an XML-parsing bug exercised through the translated
                 libexpat crate, not a standalone C-to-Rust translation fixture in the usual slate sense */
  }

Actual repro is via transpiled/libexpat directly, not a plain slate fixture (this bug lives in a specific hand-written switch shape inside the *translated* xmltok_impl.c-derived code, not in a small isolated C snippet slate's normal fixture pipeline would need to translate fresh -- though a minimal C fixture reproducing the same `goto`-into-a-later-loop *shape* directly, independent of libexpat, would be the right way to pin the lowering fix and should be written first per project convention, e.g.:

  int classify(int c) {
    switch (c) {
    case 1:
    case 2:
      break;
    case 3:
      goto low;
    case 4:
      goto high;
    default:
      return -1;
    }
    for (;;) {
      switch (c) {
      case 5: low: return 100;
      case 6: high: return 200;
      default: return -2;
      }
    }
  }

  -- calling classify(3) should return 100, classify(4) should return 200; today these would very likely fall through to the wrong branch the same way BT_SOL/BT_GT do in scanAtts.)

Root-caused while verifying slate-0nuo's setjmp/longjmp label-escape fix against the real libexpat corpus. Original symptom: transpiled/libexpat's runtests binary hangs indefinitely (100% CPU, no progress) shortly into test_nsalloc_long_element, where upstream's C runtests completes all 4836 checks near-instantly.

INVESTIGATION (bisected via targeted eprintln!/panic! instrumentation added to a scratch build of transpiled/libexpat, iterating on ~10k-iteration thresholds rather than waiting out long timeouts):
- gdb backtrace (spawned run_tests as gdb's own child -- ptrace_scope=1 blocks attaching to an unrelated PID) initially showed the hang inside `libexpat::xmltok::normal_contentTok`, called via doContent <- contentProcessor <- doProlog.
- Instrumenting doContent's own dispatch loop and normal_contentTok's own scanning loop with bounded iteration counters showed *neither* was spinning (both stayed at low iteration counts with no growth) -- the hang was in a *callee* of normal_contentTok's caller chain that itself never returns, so gdb's sampled PC (likely from an inlined callee) pointed at contentTok's frame.
- Instrumenting `normal_scanLt`'s loops the same way isolated it precisely: the inner "skip whitespace after start-tag name" loop (tests/xmltok.rs, generated from lib/xmltok_impl.c:783-799's `while (HAS_CHAR(...)) { switch (BYTE_TYPE(...)) { ... case BT_S: case BT_CR: case BT_LF: ptr += MINBPC(enc); continue; ... } return scanAtts(...); }`) spins forever one byte at a time without ever re-fetching the byte type or re-checking `ptr < end`.

ROOT CAUSE (confirmed by reading src/lower.rs):
- `lower_switch` (src/lower.rs:7757) lowers a `switch` into `'__switchN: loop { match __switch_caseN { ... } }` so fallthrough can be expressed as `continue '__switchN'`. It pushes a `LoopFrame { is_loop: false, .. }` onto `self.loop_stack` while lowering case bodies, so `lower_continue` (src/lower.rs:7915) correctly *skips* this frame when resolving what a C-level `continue` inside the switch should target -- that part works.
- The bug is in `lower_while`/`lower_while_loop_body` (src/lower.rs:7879-7886 and 7743-7755): unlike `lower_for` (7857) and `lower_do` (7888), which call `region_has_direct_continue` to decide whether the loop needs a synthetic `__loopN`/`__continueN` label pair (and, when it does, wrap the body in a `Stmt::LabeledBlock` per `lower_for_loop_body`/`lower_do_loop_body`, 7674-7742), `lower_while` *always* emits `Stmt::Loop { label: None, .. }` and `lower_while_loop_body` *always* pushes `LoopFrame { break_label: None, continue_label: None, is_loop: true }` -- there is no while-loop equivalent of the label-assignment logic `for`/`do` already have.
- Consequence: when `lower_continue` (7915-7926) walks `loop_stack.iter().rev().find(|frame| frame.is_loop)` and finds the enclosing `while`'s frame with `continue_label: None`, it falls back to emitting a bare `Stmt::Continue(None)`. That bare `continue` is textually nested inside the switch's synthetic `'__switchN: loop {}` (from the *first* bullet above), so at codegen time it binds to the *switch's own loop* instead of the intended `while` loop -- re-dispatching the same match arm forever with `__switch_caseN` never reset, hence `ptr` incrementing by exactly 1 every trip through that arm without ever re-entering the while loop's own condition check or re-fetching the byte type.
- This only manifests when something else (a switch, or potentially another unlabeled synthetic loop) sits lexically between the `continue` and its true `while` target; a `continue` directly in a while loop's own body with nothing intervening happens to work today by accident, since the nearest Rust loop *is* the right one.

FIX SHAPE: bring `lower_while`/`lower_while_loop_body` to parity with `lower_for`/`lower_for_loop_body` (7674-7707) -- compute `(break_label, continue_label)` via `region_has_direct_continue(&op.regions[1])` in `lower_while`, thread `continue_label` into `lower_while_loop_body`, and when `Some`, wrap the loop body in a `Stmt::LabeledBlock` (the `'__continueN: { ... }` pattern) exactly as `lower_for_loop_body` does, pushing a `LoopFrame` with that `continue_label` so `lower_continue` emits `break '__continueN'` instead of a bare `continue`.

Minimal repro shape (write as the fixture, per project convention -- start with a C fixture, run it through the pipeline before touching lowering code):

  while (cond) {
    switch (x) {
      case A:
        y++;
        continue;   // should continue the while, not re-dispatch the switch
      default:
        break;
    }
    do_something_that_should_be_skipped_on_case_A();
  }

Expect today: infinite loop in the generated Rust (bare `continue` rebinding to the switch's synthetic loop). After the fix: `continue 'label'` targeting the while loop correctly.

design:
Repro (single-hop works, two-hop fails) -- create these three files and translate main.c with SLATE_CLANG_ARGS=-std=c23 -nostdlibinc -isystem <dir>:

types.h:
#ifndef TYPES_H
#define TYPES_H
typedef struct {
  int __count;
  union { int __wch; char __wchb[4]; } __value;
} __mystate_t;
#endif

wrapper.h:
#ifndef WRAPPER_H
#define WRAPPER_H
#include "types.h"
#endif

main.c:
#include "wrapper.h"
int main(void) { __mystate_t s = {0}; return s.__count; }

Translating main.c directly including types.h works (union gets emitted with a real definition). Translating via wrapper.h (one extra hop) drops the union's definition while still referencing its generated name in the __mystate_t struct's field. Confirmed via a real case: tests/fixtures/uchar_conversions.c's mbstate_t chain (uchar.h -> bits/types.h) hit this exactly, which is why uchar.h's mbrtoc16/c16rtomb/mbrtoc32/c32rtomb declarations (and the __NEED_mbstate_t indirection through bits/types.h) were reverted back to their pre-C23-commit inline mbstate_t definition rather than shipped broken -- see libc-shim/include/uchar.h history (commit 944cc1c added the bits/types.h indirection; it was partially reverted in a follow-up session to keep uchar_conversions passing until this bug is fixed). Once fixed, uchar.h's mbrtoc16 block can be re-gated on __STDC_VERSION__ >= 201112L again and mbstate_t can go back through bits/types.h's __NEED_mbstate_t if desired, matching stdio.h's fpos_t/other __NEED_x consumers of the same struct. Recommended fix direction (per user preference during triage): reconcile by field position, not by name -- extend reconcile_anonymous_member_types's existing positional CIR field-type matching (currently only rewrites fields literally named __slate_anon_{index}) to also repair a field whose Type::Custom name doesn't correspond to any record actually present in the final records map, using CIR's own field-type list at that position to find/attach the right definition instead of requiring exact name equality up front.

