Why: assert() lowers correctly today (libc-shim's assert.h expands to abort(), see slate-qibg.1) but baseline lowering emits an information-losing shape: a dead let-bound if/else where the true arm yields a pure literal and the false arm calls the no-return abort() extern followed by unreachable dead code, e.g.:

let _v3: bool = argc == 2;
let _v4: i32 = if _v3 {
    let _v5: i32 = 0;
    _v5
} else {
    unsafe { abort() };
    let _v6: i32 = 0;
    _v6
};

This is the shim's (void)(cond ? 0 : (abort(), 0)) assert expansion. It should idiomatize to assert!(cond);. Since NDEBUG is already resolved at C-preprocessing time before lowering, this structural rewrite is unconditionally safe for any code matching the shape, not just source written as assert(...) — no macro-provenance check needed.

What to do:
- Add a query-driven fixup under src/fixups/query/rules/ (see docs/writing-a-query-fixup.md) matching this single-statement region:
  - a let binding whose result has zero later uses (dead binding)
  - initializer is an if/else where one arm is a pure literal and the other arm's first statement is a call to the no-arg, never-returning abort() extern
- Replace the whole let+if statement with an assert!(cond); expression statement (negate cond first if the diverging arm is the true-arm rather than the false-arm).
- Keep this optional-in-spirit per project convention: baseline lowering (already correct, closed in slate-qibg.1) must keep working with the fixup disabled.

Acceptance Criteria:
- New/updated differential fixtures under tests/fixtures/ assert accepted-case output shape (assert!( present) for both a failing and passing runtime condition, plus confirm the fixup does not misfire on the existing compile-time-provable-true case (already folds away entirely, no if/abort shape to match) and on unrelated user code that manually calls abort() without matching the exact dead-binding/pure-literal shape.
- cargo nextest r --release, cargo fmt, and cargo clippy --all-targets all clean.
- docs/passes.md updated with the new pass in the catalog.

## Why
V0 lowers `while (fgets(buf, sizeof buf, stdin) != NULL) { ... }` (commonly followed by a manual `strlen` + trailing-\n strip) to raw unsafe fgets/strlen calls over a fixed-size aligned byte array, e.g.:

```rust
let mut line: aligned::Aligned<aligned::A16, [i8; 128]> = aligned::Aligned([0; 128]);
loop {
    let _v3 = unsafe { fgets(line.as_mut_ptr() as *mut i8, 128, stdin as *mut libc::FILE) };
    if _v3.is_null() { break; }
    let _v6 = unsafe { strlen(line.as_mut_ptr() as *mut i8) };
    // manual trailing-\n strip via indexing
}
```

This is the highest-value stdin idiom to recover: it removes a real truncation/safety footgun (fixed buffer size) as well as unsafe+libc, and the manual newline-strip becomes dead code once `.lines()` already strips it.

## What to do
- Recognize the fgets-on-`stdin` read-loop shape via a facts collector (src/fixups/facts) keyed on `Known` identity for `stdin` + `fgets`, distinct from arbitrary owned FILE* handles (tracked separately, not in scope here).
- Detect and drop the redundant manual trailing-\n strip idiom (index/compare/assign against the last strlen'd byte) when it follows the loop's buffer.
- Rewrite to `for line in std::io::stdin().lock().lines() { let line = line.unwrap(); ... }`, substituting the original buffer variable's uses inside the loop body with `line`.
- Must not fire when the FILE* is not source-provably `stdin` (opened/owned handles stay on the existing fgets path).

## Acceptance Criteria
- New differential fixture(s) under tests/fixtures/ exercising: plain line echo, trailing-newline strip pattern, and a non-stdin fgets loop that must NOT be rewritten (regression guard).
- cargo nextest r --release passes.
- Fixup is independently toggleable; disabling it still yields correct (if uglier) Rust.

Why: Clang's CIR codegen hoists ALL entry-block allocas (including anonymous compiler temps, not just real C locals) to the top of the function, and reuses the same debug 'name' attribute (e.g. "tmp") for structurally-independent temps -- most visibly the result-temp GNU statement-expressions (({ ... })) use to smuggle their trailing expression's value out of the block. Two SEPARATE, non-overlapping statement-expressions in the same function that resolve to different types (e.g. one long, one unsigned long) each get their own CIR alloca, both named "tmp" but with distinct SSA results (confirmed via emit-cir: %2 = alloca ... name="tmp" -> !s64i, %4 = alloca ... name="tmp" -> !u64i, both at entry-block level, both BEFORE either statement-expression's own cir.scope region).

src/lower.rs's lower_alloca derives the Rust identifier purely via sanitize_ident(attr_str(op, "name")...) with no check for whether that name is already bound to a DIFFERENT (still-live-for-later-code) SSA slot. Both allocas above get the literal identifier "tmp", so the lowerer emits:
  let mut tmp: i64 = 0;
  let mut tmp: u64 = 0;
both hoisted adjacently at the top of the function (matching CIR's entry-block order), BEFORE any of the interleaved executable code that references either one. Because Rust's let-shadowing takes effect immediately at the second declaration, ALL subsequent code -- including the FIRST statement-expression's own body, which is emitted textually AFTER both declarations -- ends up reading/writing the SECOND (u64) binding, even where it was meant to target the FIRST (i64) one. Result: 'tmp = x;' where x: i64 assigned into a now-u64 tmp -- a genuine E0308 type mismatch in the generated Rust, not just a style/naming issue.

Minimal repro (translate and note the produced Rust fails to typecheck -- 'tmp = x;' assigns an i64 into a u64 binding):
  int main() {
    int a = ({ long x = 5; x; });
    int b = ({ unsigned long y = 6; y; });
    return a + b;
  }
Confirmed present even with SLATE_RAW_LOWER=1 (i.e. this is a lowering bug, not a later fixup pass -- dead_locals/zero_init etc were investigated and ruled out; they only obscure the symptom further downstream by discarding what looks like a dead re-declaration).

Real-world instance: tests/fixtures.chibicc/unsupported/alignof/alignof.c has 15 same-named 'tmp' allocas across its many ASSERT(...) statement-expressions (5 !s64i, 10 !u64i in original source order) all collapsing the same way.

What: lower_alloca needs to detect a collision -- an incoming CIR alloca whose sanitized name already has an entry in self.slots pointing at a DIFFERENT, still-referenced SSA value -- and mint a disambiguated Rust name for the new one (e.g. a numeric suffix, mirroring how compiler temps elsewhere in this file are named uniquely). Care needed: must not disambiguate genuinely-nested, non-overlapping same-named locals that rely on ordinary Rust block-scoping today (e.g. repeated 'x' in separate { } scope blocks, which currently work correctly and must keep working) -- the distinguishing signal is that the colliding allocas are BOTH at the same (entry-block, hoisted) nesting level rather than in disjoint nested scopes.

Why: tests/fixtures/atomics.c translates correctly, but the generated Rust (tests/fixtures.generated/atomics.rs) is far from dense. Clang materializes per-call .atomictmp/atomic-temp allocas that Slate hoists as named locals, producing ~15 shadowed 'let mut _atomictmp'/'let mut atomic_temp' declarations at fn top, with the first store's value (100) fragilely fused into the LAST shadow's initializer by zero_init. Every atomic op re-derives 'unsafe { AtomicI32::from_ptr(std::ptr::addr_of_mut!(a)) }' even though 'a' is a non-escaping local. Results bounce through atomic_temp before reaching the named local. compare_exchange expands to a 4-temp chain (Result match + is_ok + double negation !_v36 + if). Unused stdatomic support items (memory_order enum, atomic_flag struct) are emitted regardless of use.

What to do:
- Fix operand/result temp forwarding for atomic ops in the baseline lowerer (src/lower.rs) so per-call CIR atomic allocas stop becoming hoisted shadowed locals.
- Then recover idiom in facts + fixups: promote non-escaping _Atomic locals to native AtomicN storage with safe method calls, inline single-use atomic result temps, densify the compare_exchange success/expected pattern, prune unused stdatomic support items.
- Extend fixture and fuzzer coverage (explicit orderings, cgen.rs + c.bnf).

Target shape for the fixture body:
    let a = AtomicI32::new(0);
    a.store(100, Ordering::SeqCst);
    let loaded = a.load(Ordering::SeqCst);
    let fa = a.fetch_add(5, Ordering::SeqCst);
    ...
    let ok = match a.compare_exchange(expected, 42, Ordering::SeqCst, Ordering::SeqCst) { Ok(_) => true, Err(v) => { expected = v; false } } as i32;

## Why
V0 lowers `getenv(name)` plus its null-check into raw pointer comparison and manual CStr decoding:

```rust
let home: *mut i8 = unsafe { getenv(c"HOME".as_ptr() as *mut i8) };
if home != std::ptr::null_mut() {
    println!("{}", unsafe { std::ffi::CStr::from_ptr(home as *const i8) }.to_string_lossy());
} else {
    println!("none");
}
```

`std::env::var` returns `Result<String, VarError>`, which maps directly onto this exact non-null/null branch shape.

## What to do
- Recognize `getenv(name)` where `name` is a compile-time-known constant string, followed by a null-check branch.
- Rewrite to `std::env::var(name)` matched with `if let Ok(home) = ... { ... } else { ... }` (or `match`), substituting the CStr-decoded uses in the non-null branch with the bound `String`.
- Must not fire when the null-check result is used beyond the simple present/absent branch (e.g. the raw pointer is stored, passed elsewhere, or compared against something other than null).

## Acceptance Criteria
- New differential fixture(s): present-var branch, absent-var branch (e.g. an env var guaranteed unset), and a rejected case (getenv result stored/passed elsewhere) that falls back to V0 unchanged.
- cargo nextest r --release passes.
- Fixup is independently toggleable.

## Why
V0 lowers `while ((c = getchar()) != EOF) { ... }` byte-scanning loops to a raw unsafe extern "C" getchar() call compared against -1:

```rust
unsafe extern "C" { fn getchar() -> i32; }
loop {
    let _v2 = unsafe { getchar() };
    c = _v2;
    if !(_v2 != -1) { break; }
    if c == 10 { count += 1; }
}
```

Lowest effort-for-value of the three stdin fixups (no buffer/truncation concern like fgets, no variadic FFI like scanf) — the only win is dropping unsafe extern C — but it's the simplest pattern to detect (single zero-arg call, no format string, no buffer) so it's cheap to land.

## What to do
- Recognize `getchar() != EOF` (and `EOF != getchar()`) loop-condition shape via facts collector, `Known`-identity gated on `getchar`.
- Rewrite to `for byte in std::io::stdin().lock().bytes() { let c = byte.unwrap() as i32; ... }`, preserving the original `c` variable's type (i32, to keep EOF-comparison-adjacent code and any downstream arithmetic on `c` working unchanged) and its uses in the loop body.
- Must not fire if `getchar`'s result is used in a way that depends on receiving actual EOF (-1) after the loop (e.g., code checking `c == EOF` post-loop) since `.bytes()` iteration has no representable EOF sentinel — bail out to V0 in that case.

## Acceptance Criteria
- New differential fixture(s): newline-counting loop (from exploration), and a case where post-loop code inspects the EOF-carrying `c` value that must NOT be rewritten (regression guard).
- cargo nextest r --release passes.
- Fixup is independently toggleable.

Rust-AST fixup that rewrites the synthesized `__slate_memchr` byte-scan loop into a slice + iterator form.

Baseline lowering emits `__slate_memchr` (src/lower.rs:~4313) as a hand-rolled index loop:

    fn __slate_memchr(s: *const c_void, c: i32, n: usize) -> *mut c_void {
        let b: u8 = c as u8;
        let bytes: *const u8 = s as *const u8;
        let mut i: usize = 0;
        while i < n {
            if unsafe { *bytes.add(i) } == b { return unsafe { bytes.add(i) } as *mut c_void; }
            i += 1;
        }
        return std::ptr::null_mut();
    }

Idiomatic target using .iter().position():

    fn __slate_memchr(s: *const c_void, c: i32, n: usize) -> *mut c_void {
        let b = c as u8;
        let bytes = s as *const u8;
        let haystack = unsafe { std::slice::from_raw_parts(bytes, n) };
        match haystack.iter().position(|&x| x == b) {
            Some(i) => unsafe { bytes.add(i) } as *mut c_void,
            None => std::ptr::null_mut(),
        }
    }

## Context
Follows the same idiomatization pattern as slate-04q.7/.4/.12. __slate_memchr is a synthesized runtime shim (not user code) built as a FnDef in src/lower.rs; the fixup could recognize the scan-loop-returning-pointer shape, or the helper's body could be emitted idiomatically at the source. n==0 must still return null; from_raw_parts requires bytes be valid for n even when n==0 (a dangling-but-aligned ptr is fine only if n==0 and libc callers pass valid s).
